+# Task: desktop identity follow-ups from wave 49 (w50 slice e) Phase: follow-ups Owner: wave-50 builder e
+
+## Status
+
+Done (2026-10-23), with the Windows update owner left `manual` (W50-e-4). Built and tested on Linux:
+the running app's name and Linux launcher id follow the build stamp's channel (canary: `Indus Canary`,
+`ai.indus.desktop.canary`), the staged `desktopName` follows it too, and the Windows config keeps
+`verifyUpdateCodeSignature` on with `signtoolOptions.publisherName` read from
+`packaging/msix/identity.json`; `dist-win.mjs` refuses a signed build while that publisher is the
+owner placeholder. Not run: a packaged canary AppImage on a real desktop, a real NSIS update on
+Windows (needs the owner's certificate subject and the first signed run). Changed outside the
+slice's own files: `apps/desktop/scripts/stage-app.mjs` (`APP_ID` export replaced by
+`desktopNameFor`), `apps/desktop/electron/main.ts` (identity line and launcher deps).
+
+Read (reference @ `ab3fe9dffb0a89d534cb795e2a2940e5f6542056`): `apps/desktop/electron/product-identity.ts`
+(how a non-stable build pins its name before the first userData read), `apps/desktop/electron/main.ts`
+(where the app name is chosen and set, the Windows app user model id), `apps/desktop/electron-builder.config.cjs`
+(`extraMetadata.desktopName`, the Windows signing block with the publisher name).
+Evidence: none.
+Vendor code read for behaviour (not the reference): electron-builder 26.17.0 `app-builder-lib`
+(`codeSign/windowsSignToolManager.js` publisher name, `publish/PublishManager.js` app-update.yml,
+`targets/LinuxTargetHelper.js` desktop entry), electron-updater 6.8.9 (`NsisUpdater.js`,
+`windowsExecutableCodeSignatureVerifier.js`).
+
+What it does (own words): wave 49 gave a canary build its own app id, product name, Linux
+executable and feed at packaging time (`scripts/channel-identity.cjs`), but the running app still
+called itself `Indus` and wrote its Linux launcher entry as `ai.indus.desktop.desktop` with icon
+`ai.indus.desktop` — so a canary AppImage overwrote the stable app's menu entry, and its windows
+were grouped under the stable launcher. Now the running app takes its identity from the build
+stamp's channel through the same derivation: a canary is named `Indus Canary` (window title, app
+menu, `app.setName`, the launcher entry's Name and StartupWMClass) and its launcher entry and icon
+are `ai.indus.desktop.canary`. The staged `package.json`'s `desktopName` (which Electron hands to
+the desktop as the app's desktop-file id) follows the stamp's channel too.
+
+Windows: electron-updater checks an NSIS update's Authenticode signer against `publisherName` in
+`app-update.yml`, and electron-builder only writes that key when it knows a publisher. Without it,
+the check is silently skipped. The config now keeps `verifyUpdateCodeSignature` on and gives
+electron-builder the channel's configured publisher (the certificate subject recorded in
+`packaging/msix/identity.json`, the same value the MSIX lane signs with). While that value is the
+owner placeholder, a signed Windows build is refused before anything is built (a feed that names a
+placeholder publisher would refuse every later update); an unsigned build keeps the placeholder,
+which can never match a real certificate (fails closed).
+
+Inputs → outputs: build stamp `channel` (packaged) → runtime name and launcher id;
+`INDUS_DESKTOP_CHANNEL` / `--channel` → config `win.signtoolOptions.publisherName`,
+`win.verifyUpdateCodeSignature: true`, staged `desktopName`; `dist-win.mjs` with an armed signer
+and a placeholder publisher → error naming the file and key.
+
+Files: `apps/desktop/scripts/channel-identity.cjs` (publisher lookup), `apps/desktop/electron-builder.config.cjs`,
+`apps/desktop/scripts/dist-win.mjs`, `apps/desktop/scripts/stage-app.mjs`, new `apps/desktop/electron/identity.ts`,
+`apps/desktop/electron/linuxLauncher.ts`, `apps/desktop/electron/main.ts`; tests beside each.
+
+Edge cases / failure modes:
+- Unpackaged (dev) run: no stamp → the stable names (`Indus`), as before; the launcher is never
+  written outside a packaged AppImage anyway.
+- Packaged with an unreadable stamp → canary names (the rule of `channel-identity.cjs`: an
+  unlabelled build never takes the stable identity), so it can never overwrite the stable entry.
+- userData is unaffected: Electron derives it from the packaged productName before main runs.
+- Unknown channel in the config env → the existing `resolveChannel` error.
+- identity.json missing, unparsable, or without a publisher for the channel → the config fails
+  naming the file (a build must not ship a feed without a publisher).
+- Placeholder publisher + no signer → build goes on (unsigned, manual update owner).
+- Placeholder publisher + armed signer → refused before the build, message names file and key only.
+
+Our design differences / decisions:
+- W50-e-1: the runtime derives its identity from the build stamp's channel (the one declaration of
+  what a build is, P20-37), not a value baked into the main bundle: `--skip-build` can reuse a
+  bundle built for another channel, the stamp is always written per build. `electron/identity.ts`
+  repeats the two base names and a test holds it equal to `channel-identity.cjs` for every channel.
+- W50-e-2: the Windows publisher is read from `packaging/msix/identity.json` (`stable` / `canary`
+  `publisher`), the one place the certificate subject is configured; no new owner value.
+- W50-e-3: a signed Windows build with a placeholder publisher is refused (as `msix.mjs` refuses to
+  sign with one); unsigned builds keep the placeholder, which fails closed.
+- W50-e-4: the stamp's update owner on Windows stays `manual`; flipping it to electron-updater waits
+  for the first signed run (owner item). Only the config half (publisher + verification on) lands.
+- Left out: the notification centre's literal app name (`ambient/install.ts`), the Windows
+  AppUserModelID, the `.deb` entry's StartupWMClass (electron-builder takes it from desktopName).
+
+Tests we will write:
+- identity: stable/canary/dev/no-stamp names; equal to `channel-identity.cjs` for every channel.
+- launcher: canary file name, icon name, Name and StartupWMClass; stable unchanged.
+- stage-app: desktopName follows the stamp's channel.
+- config: `verifyUpdateCodeSignature` true and `publisherName` = identity.json's publisher per
+  channel; a missing publisher throws; schema still valid.
+- dist-win: armed signer + placeholder → refused; complete publisher or no signer → goes on.
+
+## Decisions for the lead
+- W50-e-1 … W50-e-4 above.
+- Owner: the certificate subject in `apps/desktop/packaging/msix/identity.json` (stable and canary
+  `publisher`) is now also the Windows update publisher; filling it is required before the first
+  signed Windows run.
+
+Compare checklist (filled after the rewrite; reopened `electron/product-identity.ts`,
+`electron/main.ts` (app name, app user model id), `electron-builder.config.cjs` (`extraMetadata`,
+Windows signing block)):
+- [x] A non-stable build takes its own display name at start-up, before windows exist; stable keeps
+  the historic name. Same here, from the stamp's channel. (test: `electron/identity.test.ts` ›
+  "a packaged canary is Indus Canary …")
+- [x] The packaged package.json's `desktopName` follows the build's app id. Same, from the stamp
+  the staged app ships. (test: `scripts/stage-app.test.ts` › "names the desktop-file id after the
+  stamp's channel …")
+- [x] One derivation for every name a channel owns. The running app's copy is held equal to
+  `channel-identity.cjs` by a test instead of importing it (W50-e-1). (test: `identity.test.ts` ›
+  "names each channel exactly as packaging does")
+- [x] The Windows signing block carries a publisher name for the update check. Ours is always set,
+  from the configured certificate subject, not from an environment variable that may be missing
+  (an absent name makes electron-updater skip the check). (tests: `scripts/windowsUpdates.test.ts`
+  › "puts each channel's configured publisher …", "electron-builder takes that publisher …")
+- [x] Gap found: nothing stopped a signed build from shipping a placeholder publisher — fixed by
+  `checkSignedPublisher` in `dist-win.mjs`, pinned by `windowsUpdates.test.ts` › "refuses a signed
+  build while the channel's publisher is a placeholder".
+- [x] Linux launcher entry (our own P20-38 feature; the reference writes none at run time): file,
+  icon, Name and StartupWMClass follow the identity. (test: `electron/linuxLauncher.test.ts` › "a
+  canary writes its own entry and icon …")
+- Deliberate differences: the reference pins userData by hand for non-stable builds; Electron
+  already derives ours from the packaged productName (wave 49 `extraMetadata`), so main does not
+  move it. The reference reads the publisher from an environment variable on the Azure route only;
+  ours reads the one configured subject on every route.
+- Not taken: the Windows app user model id (we set none today; follow-up), a test-only environment
+  override of the app name, the notification centre's app name (`ambient/install.ts`, follow-up).
